<!--META-DATA
{
	"tags": ["statistics", "confidence-interval", "proportion", "function", "table"]
}
-->

<statement>
	<p>
		This function computes and displays a formatted confidence interval table for estimated proportions based on observed category counts. It outputs the proportion, percentage, margin of error, and confidence level for each variable.
	</p>

	<tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
		<col width="12%"/>
		<col width="88%" halign="left"/>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Inputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p><pf>varNames</pf> (m x 1) cell array or string array — names or labels for each category.</p>
				<p><pf>varCounts</pf> (m x 1) numeric vector — observed counts for each category.</p>
				<p><pf>N</pf> (1 x 1) integer — total number of observations or trials.</p>
				<p><pf>CL</pf> (optional, 1 x 1 integer) — desired confidence level (in percent). Default is 95.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Outputs:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>Displays a MATLAB table with columns:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><pf>varNames</pf> — category names.</li>
					<li><pf>varCounts</pf> — observed counts.</li>
					<li><pf>pHat</pf> — estimated proportions (rounded to four decimals).</li>
					<li><pf>pct</pf> — formatted percentage values.</li>
					<li><pf>marginErr</pf> — confidence interval margin of error (± percentage form).</li>
					<li><pf>confLvl</pf> — reported confidence level for each row.</li>
				</ul>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell><p>Details:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ The function supports confidence levels of 99%, 95%, 90%, 80%, and 70%. The nearest available level is used if <pf>CL</pf> does not exactly match one of these.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The corresponding z-scores are assigned as follows:</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell>
				<p>
					<sidebyside widths="10% 10% 10% 10% 10%" margins="10% 10%">
						<p>99% → 2.575</p>
						<p>95% → 1.960</p>
						<p>90% → 1.645</p>
						<p>80% → 1.281</p>
						<p>70% → 1.036</p>
					</sidebyside>
				</p>
			</cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The sample proportions are computed as <m>\hat{p} = \text{varCounts} / N</m>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The standard error for each estimate is <m>\text{SE} = \sqrt{ \hat{p}(1 - \hat{p}) / N }</m>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row>
			<cell/>
			<cell><p>▸ The margin of error is <m>\text{ME} = z \times \text{SE}</m>, and the corresponding interval is <m>\hat{p} \pm \text{ME}</m>.</p></cell>
		</row>

		<row bottom="minor">
			<cell><p>Notes:</p></cell>
			<cell>
				<p>▸ The displayed table summarizes confidence intervals in an easy-to-read format with percentages.</p>
				<p>▸ The function does not return an output variable; it directly prints the results using <pf>disp</pf>.</p>
			</cell>
		</row>
	</tabular>

	<p>
		Examples:
		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 1: Compute 95% confidence intervals for survey responses
				varNames  = ["Yes"; "No"; "Undecided"];
				varCounts = [520; 430; 50];
				N = sum(varCounts);
				display_results(varNames, varCounts, N)
				% Displays table with estimated proportions, margins of error, and 95% CI.
			</code>
		</program>

		<program>
			<code>
				% Example 2: 99% confidence level for categorical data
				varNames  = ["Success"; "Failure"];
				varCounts = [240; 60];
				display_results(varNames, varCounts, 300, 99)
				% Displays table using z = 2.575 for 99% confidence level.
			</code>
		</program>
	</p>
</statement>
